  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures persist in the Long Term period as our \nregion lies along the western edge of an upper ridge through much of \nthe week. The ridge begins to shift west back toward our area \nWednesday night, flattening as it does so. Given ridging remains our \nprimary weather maker, afternoon highs will largely top out in the \n90s each afternoon this week. Triple digit heat is forecast over the \nnortheastern Permian Basin and near/along the river valleys Tuesday, \nbecoming restricted to the Rio Grande valley the remainder of the \nweek. Meanwhile, temperatures in and near the higher elevations look \nto reach the 80s most afternoons. Overnight lows shall range within \nthe 60s to 70s, with 60s eventually becoming more prevalent by the \nweekend. Sometime over the weekend, long-range models show a cold \nfront dropping down from the Panhandle. However, there is still \nquite a bit of uncertainty regarding details, so stay tuned! With \nany luck, we may finally be able to cool down to more seasonable \nconditions. Otherwise, rain chances are in the forecast most \nafternoons, mainly over the western portions of our area. These come \ncourtesy of upslope flow combined with lift from disturbances \ndrifting along the edge of the ridge.",
